// Test: the dictionary — decisions 7 to 11 of Divergente_ES/forma/README.md.
//
// The named tuple HAS the dictionary model. There is no new type — what changed
// is the vocabulary, since a tuple is immutable by definition and this is not.
// The notation came later, in the same release: `#(clave: valor)` is the
// dictionary and `(1, 2)` the positional tuple, because the colon alone could
// not tell an EMPTY one from the other (see dict_marcado.zy).
//
// Before this it was a RECORD, not a dictionary: readable only when the program
// already knew what it held. Five things were missing, and each one alone was
// enough to keep a JSON built piece by piece from being built at all.

u = #(nombre: "Ana", edad: 30)

// ── the key may be COMPUTED — the piece that made it a dictionary (DM-09) ────
// It used to be `array index must be Int, got String` in both Rust engines and
// an empty line in the browser one, which was the worst of the three.
k = "edad"
>> u[k] ¶
>> u["nombre"] ¶

// The dot and the bracket are the same lookup; the bracket reaches keys the dot
// cannot spell, which is what JSON needs.
>> u.nombre ¶
u["dos palabras"]$~ 7
>> u["dos palabras"] ¶

// ── a key that is NOT there gets added (decision 7) ─────────────────────────
// Note the contrast with the array, where `arr[7]$~ v` fails (decision 13). The
// two are not inconsistent: an array is addressed by POSITION, so writing past
// the end would leave a hole; a dictionary is addressed by KEY and has none.
u["ciudad"]$~ "Lima"
>> u ¶

// ── ask before reading (decision 10) ────────────────────────────────────────
// On a dictionary `$?` asks about the KEY, as `in` does in Python and in JS.
// Reading an absent key is an error, so without this there is no way to consult
// a dictionary built piece by piece.
>> (u$? "ciudad") ¶
>> (u$? "sueldo") ¶

// ── an absent key is ##Key, not ##_ and not an empty value ──────────────────
// Python's KeyError, not JavaScript's undefined — coherent with `a[0]`, which is
// also an error rather than a silently wrong answer.
!? {
    >> u["sueldo"] ¶
} :! ##Key {
    >> "##Key capturado" ¶
}

// ── walked by KEY, in insertion order (decision 8) ──────────────────────────
// `for k in d`, as Python spells it. With `d[k]` available the key is enough to
// reach the value, so no destructuring pattern has to enter `@`.
d = #(alfa: 10, beta: 20, gamma: 30)
@ clave:d {
    >> "  " clave " = " d[clave] ¶
}

// ── a key is removed by its ADDRESS (decision 9) ────────────────────────────
// `$-[…]` already means "remove by address" for the array (`arr$-[1]`, by
// position). In a dictionary the address IS the key, so it is the same operator
// with the same sense — and `$- valor` stays free to mean "by value" in both.
d$-["beta"]
>> d ¶

// ── nesting: this is already JSON, which is why no new type was needed ──────
config = #(
    servidor: #(host: "localhost", puerto: 8080),
    etiquetas: ["web", "api"]
)
>> config.servidor.host ¶
>> config["servidor"]["puerto"] ¶
>> config.etiquetas[1] ¶

// The positional read `d[2]` was withdrawn (decision 11) — see
// reject/collections/02_positional_read_dict.zy. The POSITIONAL tuple keeps
// `t[1]` in full: there the index is the only address there is.
